\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{One Morris–Lecar trajectory}
\date{2026-11-25}

\p{Take the velocity of \ref{fnip-0005} in the Hopf column at
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}=60}. The rest point #{(V_{R},n_{R})} is the
point at which both components of that velocity vanish.
The [trajectory](fnip-0009) below starts at #{(0,n_{R})}. The voltage
rises and then falls, and the pair returns to the rest point.
\citet{Figure 3.1a, pp. 50–51}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
is this regime: after a sufficiently large displacement the voltage
makes one excursion and the state returns to rest. The initial
voltage #{0} is the displacement chosen for this solution.}

